now to a cbs news investigation of the  country's largest online mental health  provider cerebral offers prescriptions  in therapy for conditions like adhd and  depression it started just two years ago  and grew rapidly during the pandemic now  the justice department is looking at  cerebral's prescribing practices in an  exclusive interview ceo dr david moe  told our consumer investigative  correspondent anna warner that cerebral  is cooperating with the doj and he's  confident that the investigation will  find no problems former patients and  employees of the company told anna other  concerns good morning good morning nate  the ceo says cerebral has saved lives  but our investigation raises questions  about how the company treats patients  some of whom told us cerebral failed  them when they were in desperate need of  care  every single night i would go to bed  just  tomorrow i'm going to do it i'm going to  be better tomorrow 28 year old rachel  coster developed severe anxiety after  the birth of her second child and then i  wake up and  i'm home with the kids by myself and  it's just overwhelming  then earlier this year she saw ads on  social media like these stigmas and  anxieties sometimes prevent people from  seeking help they were for a company  called cerebral that offers affordable  mental health care services online  koster signed up met with her prescriber  online and got a prescription all in a  single day but after that every time i  needed her help she was never available  like when koster needed instructions  from her prescriber on how to safely  switch to the new medication they gave  her i didn't know what i was supposed to  do  she followed up again and again six days  later and still no answer koster started  the new drug then later she broke out in  a rash a potential side effect for which  the drugs maker says patients should  call their doctor right away and i  messaged back letting them know it was  spreading and getting worse  and they said that they were still  trying to get a hold of that prescriber  she finally went to the emergency room  where doctors treated her with iv  steroids and told her to stop taking the  medication she then canceled her  cerebral membership they make it seem  like they want to help and then they get  you  and they're gone  koster is one of over a dozen patients  who told cbs news they had problems with  cerebral's quality of care dr david moe  is cerebral's former chief medical  officer and new ceo he says their system  works if you look at our outcomes and  this is what's most important do the  patients get better  they do they absolutely do  but documents obtained by cbs news show  cerebral leadership was informed about  risks the company was facing including  patient and clinical safety issues hires  who may not meet our hiring standards  and staff practicing with expired or  suspended licenses  many customers complain that they like  koster couldn't reach their prescribers  to which dr mo responds the average wait  time to get to a prescriber is days and  that's i think what matters the most yet  this internal log we obtain shows the  company's own staffers flagged nearly  1200 instances of prescribers being  unresponsive over the past 11 months  wouldn't it be dangerous if patients  cannot get hold of their prescribers and  therefore can't get their medications or  they're not using them correctly  so i will say we'll definitely take a  look into this and very we're very  serious about continuous improvement  here  but it's not just patients complaining  it's like a fast food restaurant get as  many people in as fast as you can  melissa butoric worked as a client coach  for cerebral a particular area of  concern for her suicidal patients do you  feel like clients who come to cerebral  who are suicidal are in safe hands no  without a doubt now for example she  points to the company's handling of  suicidal clients through the messaging  app called slack dr mo says the system  allows staff to quickly respond to  suicidal patients within minutes a  crisis specialist would help triage this  patient to the most appropriate  level of care but one staffer messaged  about not getting a response from the  crisis team another wrote that a  suicidal patient had written into chat  last week and no one had written back  it's chaotic it's confusing it could be  extremely dangerous right if you  somebody misses the message or doesn't  follow it the words she said were  exactly hey i'm i'm going to kill myself  this former cerebral phone coordinator  who didn't want to show his face because  he fears reprisal told us he handled  calls from suicidal patients despite  having minimal training i'm not trained  i don't want to say the wrong thing and  i didn't want that on my conscience let  alone anybody to die because of  something i said wrong  i would have to review this case what i  will say is this we've invested a lot of  resources and for the most part as you  can tell by our aggregate numbers we're  able to save lives you have to take this  in aggregate and uh where many of our  patients do really well if you look at  our ratings but these people aren't  living in aggregate right you're talking  about individual lives  here  i would hope that  so  i refer  my close friends my family members to  use cerebral our commitment here is the  quality of care and now that i'm ceo  we're going to double down and triple  down on that thesis  now after our interview referring to the  internal logging of prescription issues  cerebral told us that shows the safety  checks and balances the company has in  place the company also says that it has  reduced suicidal thinking in 50 of  patients that it's studied in their own  study also after our interview the  company apologized to rachel koster  saying its clinician did not follow  their procedures and is no longer  working with cerebral and if you have a  mental health story to share with us  please send me an email werner a at  cbsnews.com last name first initial but  that some of the concerns we're hearing  from patients former employees former  clintons you know i give the ceo though  credit for speaking to you because a lot  of people in this situation are doing  the jujitsu move and you know turning  into teenage mutant ninja turtles not  wanting to talk to you so the fact that  he did sit down with you i give him a  lot but there's still so many questions  there are some flaws yes  yeah they're troubling though they've  said there are some issues that they are  working on um he has said very clearly  that he is dedicated to working on them  and that he prioritizes quality of care  above all else well he has work to do  terrific work and great stories thank  you  thank you very much
 

